  "strategy": "Approach and Rationale:\nOur approach leverages a combination of multi-scale optimization and derivative-free local search to escape local optima. We represent the function h using a weighted Fourier series expansion, which allows more diversity in the solution landscape than direct grid optimization. The Fourier representation enables efficient computation of the overlap integral via the Poisson summation formula, reducing the effective dimensionality and capturing both smooth and oscillatory behavior. This allows us to escape local optima that are common in grid space optimization.\n\nThe core idea is to parameterize h as:\n  h(x) = (1/\u03c0) * sum_{k=-N}^{N} a_k * sin(2\u03c0k x) + 0.5 + \u03b5\nwith scaling to ensure integral=1 and bounds. This forms an underdetermined system with inherent regularization, allowing exploration of non-local structures.\n\nMathematical Specification:\nRepresent h on [0,2] using Fourier series:\n  h(x) = A + sum_{k=1}^{M} (a_k * sin(2\u03c0k x) + b_k * cos(2\u03c0k x))\n\nThe integral constraint is:\n  \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x) dx = 1\n\nThe objective is:\n  C = max\u2096 \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx\n\nThe Fourier representations naturally satisfy the integral constraint by construction. The overlap integral can be computed using Poisson summation, converting it into a discrete sum over characteristic functions.\n\nImplementation Procedure:\n1. Initialization:\n   - Start with parent's symmetry axis-aligned initialization at resolutions 256, 512, and 1024 using Fourier projection\n   - For each n-points:\n        n = 256, 512, 1024\n        Let F = floor(n/2) Fourier coefficients to optimize\n        a = np.random.randn(F) * 0.1, b = np.random.randn(F) * 0.1\n        Project to satisfy integral=1 via Poisson weighting\n\n2. Multi-Scale Reduction:\n   For each resolution, use:\n    - FastFourierTransform for base representation\n    - Gradient-free optimizer (Nelder-Mead) with 50 random restarts\n    - Use momentum-based adaptive sampling in coefficient space\n\n3. Local Refinement:\n   After finding local minima at resolution n, upsample to 2n points using:\n        Poisson interpolation and Fourier mode analysis\n\n4. Entropy-Preserving Refinement:\n   Periodically inject random noise perturbations to escape local minima:\n        New coefficients = Old coefficients + \u03b5 * exp(i k) (complex Fourier noise)\n\n5. Error Checking:\n   Verify integral constraint with:\n        error = |\u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x) dx - 1| < 1e-6\n   For coefficient optimization, check:\n        all_coeff.norm < 0.5 * standard-deviation (controls smoothness)\n\n6. Final Output:\n   For each local minimum found, compute exact overlap integral (not approximated) using:\n        Compute convolution via FFT then scale by dx=2/n\n\nValidation and Return Contract:\n1. For each candidate, output:\n   - h_values array of length n_points (the resolution used)\n   - c5_bound = computed maximum correlation * dx\n   - n_points (the resolution used)\n\n2. Ensure feasibility checks:\n   Feasible if:\n        - Sum(h_values * dx) \u2248 1 (tol=1e-5)\n        - All 0 \u2264 h_values \u2264 1\n        - Computed overlap integral is well-defined\n\n3. Optimization Parameters:\n   - Primary frequency limit: M = min(floor(sqrt(n)), 100)\n   - Coefficient warmup: 1/4 of initial range then exp-descent\n   - Budget allocation: 70% to multi-level search, 10% to upsampling, 20% to noise injection\n\n4. Resolution Specification:\n   - Start with 256 points\n   - If time permits upsample to 1024 points\n   - The function transforms grid via Fourier projection, maintaining integral\n\nSearch Choices and Variations:\n1. Initialization Strategies:\n   a) Symmetric mode initialization (basis functions with even/odd symmetry)\n   b) Random perturbation followed by projection\n   c) Low-frequency initialization then high-frequency injection\n   \n2. Frequency Control:\n   Use relative frequency weighting: \n        effective_weights = |coef|^p / (rank+1)^q \n   p=0.5 for high-frequency regularization, q=1 for symmetry penalty\n\n3. Optimization Methods:\n   - For stable modes: use L-BFGS-B with analytical gradient\n   - For resistant modes: use coordinate descent with adaptive intervals\n   - For boundary effects: use reflection principle with exact gradient calculation at boundaries\n\n4. Practical Estimators:\n   - Main objective: Exact FFT-based convolution (mode=\"full\")\n   - Secondary estimates: Use cyclic group convolution for comparison\n   - Tertiary fallback: Use random grid sampling for O(n log n) estimation\n\nCompute Budget and Robustness:\n- Primary operations: O(M log M) per grid point\n- Worst-case M=200, grid=1024 gives ~200*log(1024)\u22482200 operations per iteration\n- 1000 iterations = 2.2e6 operations (each ~2000 FLOPS better estimate) = 1.1e6 operations (assuming efficiency)\n- 1100 second budget: at 1e6 operations per second, completes 110 comparisons annually?\n\nUnder typical assumptions, we should find at least one improvement.\n\nBut note: The initial solution gave C ~ 0.3808. We need to find a better solution.\n\n<span class=\"log\">(Proposal made)</span>",
